WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.782
Secret storage and secret delivery are connected.

2
00:00:02.782 --> 00:00:11.525
A secret may be protected in a vault but still exposed when it is passed to an application through an unsafe channel.

3
00:00:11.525 --> 00:00:18.281
C++ applications often receive configuration from files, environment variables, command-line arguments, service managers, container platforms, CI

4
00:00:18.281 --> 00:00:21.063
systems, deployment pipelines, or runtime injection mechanisms.

5
00:00:21.063 --> 00:00:24.640
Each path has different visibility, inheritance, and logging behavior.

6
00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:31.396
Command-line secrets can be visible to process inspection tools, service metadata, shell history, or diagnostic collection.

7
00:00:31.396 --> 00:00:40.139
Environment variables are convenient, but they may be inherited by child processes, copied into crash reports, or surfaced by debugging and support

8
00:00:40.139 --> 00:00:40.537
tooling.

9
00:00:40.537 --> 00:00:45.703
Configuration files require strict permissions, controlled paths, integrity checks, and careful backup behavior.

10
00:00:45.703 --> 00:00:54.049
CI and deployment systems can provide safer injection when configured correctly, but they can also leak values through console output, build

11
00:00:54.049 --> 00:00:56.434
artifacts, or overly broad project permissions.

12
00:00:56.434 --> 00:01:03.985
Applications should document which delivery mechanisms are approved for each environment and reject unsafe alternatives in production where possible.

13
00:01:03.985 --> 00:01:07.959
The delivery path should match the secret type and platform.

14
00:01:07.959 --> 00:01:16.702
Local development may use a different controlled mechanism than production, but it should not train developers to paste real credentials into source

15
00:01:16.702 --> 00:01:18.292
files or command lines.

16
00:01:18.292 --> 00:01:25.445
Clear startup diagnostics can report that a required secret is missing or inaccessible without printing the value itself.

17
00:01:25.445 --> 00:01:33.394
Deployment reviews should also confirm that secrets are not copied into images, templates, or generated configuration that outlives the intended

18
00:01:33.394 --> 00:01:33.791
release.

19
00:01:33.791 --> 00:01:38.560
That combination of approved delivery, redaction, and explicit failure reduces accidental exposure.
